




#headingASTRONAUT HEALTH MONITORING APP
Evidence Review and Prototype Design Brief






 Executive summary
The proposed application will be designed trend-visualization prototype—not as an autonomous diagnostic system. Its role is to combine astronaut-entered observations, exercise and wearable-sensor summaries, clinician-provided laboratory results, and radiation-dosimeter records into a single longitudinal dashboard. It can flag missing data, meaningful changes from an individual baseline, and predefined review thresholds. It cannot infer bone density, diagnose infection, quantify immune competence, or measure radiation dose from ordinary fitness sensors alone.
The three health domains are connected. Microgravity reduces mechanical loading and contributes to bone and muscle deconditioning; spaceflight stressors including altered sleep, psychological stress, microgravity, and radiation can alter immune responses; ionizing radiation adds a separate exposure hazard that requires dedicated dosimetry and mission-level radiation protection. An integrated app can make these data easier to interpret, but the validity of its alerts depends on data quality, calibrated devices, clinical context, and human review.
Prototype design idea:
•	Build a dashboard with domain cards (Musculoskeletal, Immune/Infection, Radiation) and other physiological factors  plus a combined timeline and data-quality status.
•	Start with real-time or daily data that a prototype can plausibly receive: activity/exercise logs, heart rate and sleep summaries from compatible wearables, symptom check-ins, and manually imported lab/dosimeter records.
•	Display trends against a personal pre-mission baseline and clearly label the source, timestamp, unit, and whether a value is measured, self-reported, imported, or modeled.
•	Use transparent rules for alerts (for example, fever reported, new symptoms, missed exercise sessions, or a dosimeter threshold provided by mission medical/radiation teams); do not invent universal clinical cut-offs.
•	Present alerts as 'review required' rather than a diagnosis, and include escalation guidance defined by the mission medical team.


1. Purpose, users, and design principles
The intended users are astronauts, flight surgeons, biomedical officers, exercise specialists, and mission operations staff. During a long-duration mission, the app should reduce the effort required to spot patterns across several data streams while preserving the distinction between a measurement and a medical conclusion.

Core design principles
•	Evidence-first: each metric must have a known source, unit, collection method, timestamp, and interpretation caveat.
•	Baseline-aware: individual changes over time may be more useful than comparison with a generic population range, but baseline comparisons do not replace clinical reference ranges.
•	Explainable: every alert should say which input triggered it, how recent the input is, and what action is recommended by the approved protocol.
•	Fail-safe: missing, stale, contradictory, or out-of-range sensor data should be marked as uncertain—not silently treated as normal.
•	Privacy and access control: health records should be encrypted, access-limited, auditable, and retained only under an agreed mission policy.

2. Bone and muscle health in microgravity

2.1 Why it matters
On Earth, everyday movement and gravity load the skeleton and postural muscles. In microgravity, weight-bearing bones receive less mechanical stimulus, while many muscles perform less work. Bone resorption can outpace bone formation, reducing bone mineral density and potentially increasing fracture risk. Muscle unloading can reduce muscle size, strength, endurance, and functional capacity. NASA summarizes average bone-density loss of roughly 1–1.5% per month in some long-duration mission contexts, particularly at weight-bearing sites; published rates vary with the skeletal site, mission, countermeasures, and measurement method. These figures should be presented as research context, not as a prediction for every astronaut.
The consequences are operational as well as medical: an astronaut may need to perform demanding tasks, operate equipment, conduct an EVA, or return to a gravity environment after months in orbit. Current exercise programs reduce but do not necessarily eliminate deconditioning. NASA uses resistance exercise, treadmill and cycle training, nutritional measures, and pre-/post-flight assessment; DXA scans and selected blood/urine biomarkers help characterize skeletal changes.
2.2 What the app can realistically monitor
Indicator	Potential data source	What the app can show	Important limitation
Exercise adherence	Astronaut log; device/session records	Completed sessions, duration, workload, missed sessions, weekly trends	Completion does not prove adequate loading or physiological benefit.
Activity and movement	Wearable accelerometer/gyroscope	Daily activity, movement trends, prolonged inactivity, recovery patterns	Motion is not a direct measure of bone density or muscle strength. Microgravity changes the meaning of familiar activity metrics.
Heart rate during exercise	Validated wearable or exercise-device export	Heart-rate response and recovery trends alongside workload	Heart rate alone cannot diagnose deconditioning; medication, hydration, stress, and sensor artefacts affect it.
Strength/function	Approved dynamometer or standardized functional test; entered results	Repeat-test trends and percentage change from personal baseline	Requires a standardized protocol and calibrated equipment; consumer sensors are not a substitute.
Bone mineral density	Clinical DXA/QCT result imported by authorized staff	Pre-/post-flight or periodic BMD values and changes at specified anatomical sites	The app cannot measure BMD with a phone or ordinary wearable. DXA/QCT requires dedicated clinical equipment.
Bone-turnover indicators	Clinician-provided blood/urine laboratory results	Longitudinal charts for selected markers and calcium-related measures	Interpretation depends on collection timing, renal function, diet, hydration, and lab method.
Nutrition and recovery	Food/fluid logs; sleep diary or wearable estimate	Intake/adherence trends and associations with exercise or symptoms	Logs may be incomplete; estimated intake and wearable sleep staging are imperfect.

2.3 Countermeasures the app can support
•	Track adherence to the approved resistance, treadmill, and cycle schedule; show workload and session completion, not simply a binary 'fit/unfit' label.
•	Prompt for missing session logs and record equipment problems, pain, unusual fatigue, or exercise intolerance for medical review.
•	Visualize approved nutritional targets and recorded intake, especially when clinical teams are tracking energy, protein, vitamin D, calcium, or fluid intake.
•	Show trends from standardized strength or functional tests when these are part of the mission protocol.
•	Import clinician-reviewed DXA/QCT and laboratory results when available, preserving anatomical site, units, collection date, and laboratory method.
2.4 Suggested dashboard and alert logic
•	Green/complete: recent exercise and measurement data are present and no configured rule is triggered.
•	Amber/review: repeated missed sessions, a sustained drop in standardized functional-test performance, new persistent musculoskeletal symptoms, or a meaningful change from baseline.
•	Red/urgent: only for predefined mission-approved triggers, such as an acute injury or a clinical result flagged by the medical team. The app should route the alert to the approved response pathway.
•	Always show the evidence behind the alert: e.g., 'Two strength assessments are lower than baseline; values and test dates shown.' Avoid claiming 'bone loss detected' unless a clinical imaging result actually supports it.

2.5 Key quantitative findings

Finding	Value reported
Femoral neck bone loss after 4 to 6 month missions (first postflight scan)	About 6.7% (± 1.3%), n = 56
Maximum hip bone mineral density loss reported during long-duration flight	Up to 26% (cited in the review as a personal communication, so treat as indicative) [verify]
Relationship to mission length	Longer missions: more loss and lower likelihood of recovery; some astronauts did not recover bone density
Effectiveness of ISS exercise countermeasures	Reduce but do not eliminate bone loss and muscle deconditioning

3. Immune health and infection risks during spaceflight
3.1 Why it matters
Spaceflight combines several stressors—microgravity, radiation, sleep and circadian disruption, isolation, workload, and psychological stress—that can alter immune-cell activity and inflammatory signalling. Studies have reported changes in immune-cell function and distribution, stress hormones, inflammatory pathways, and reactivation or shedding of latent viruses. NASA reports that astronauts have experienced issues such as cold sores, rashes, atypical allergies, and infections. These observations do not mean every astronaut becomes immunocompromised; effects differ by person, mission, timing, and the test used. The mechanisms and clinical significance of some findings remain active research questions.
In a deep-space mission, delayed evacuation and limited diagnostic capacity raise the stakes. An app could help crews record symptoms early and make available trends visible to clinicians, but it cannot establish immune competence from symptoms or a single blood count.

3.2 Symptoms and data that may be relevant
•	Self-reported symptoms: fever or chills, sore throat, cough, nasal symptoms, skin rash, cold sores, gastrointestinal symptoms, pain, unusual fatigue, or a new wound/skin change.
•	Context: onset time, duration, severity, recurrence, contact/exposure history if required by protocol, medications, sleep, stress, and recent procedures.
•	Basic physiological context: temperature, heart rate, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation when clinically indicated and measured with suitable devices. These are nonspecific signals, not infection diagnoses.
•	Clinician-ordered laboratory data: complete blood count with differential (including white blood cell and lymphocyte counts), selected inflammatory markers such as CRP, pathogen-specific tests, and research immune biomarkers when available.
•	Research-oriented samples: blood, saliva, or urine can be used in approved protocols to investigate immune changes, stress hormones, and viral reactivation. Some assays require specialized handling and cannot be run by a simple app.

3.3 What the app can realistically monitor
Data stream	Collection method	App function	Limitations
Daily symptom check-in	Structured self-report	Symptom timeline, severity, duration, recurrence, escalation based on approved protocol	Subjective and dependent on reporting; absence of symptoms does not exclude infection.
Body temperature	Validated thermometer; manual/device import	Trend display and protocol-based fever flag	Measurement site/device accuracy matters; fever thresholds must follow mission medical guidance.
CBC/differential and labs	Imported laboratory report	Trend absolute values, compare with clinician-defined reference limits, flag missing follow-up	A normal CBC does not rule out infection or immune dysfunction; ranges vary by lab and context.
Saliva/viral assays	Specialized sample and assay; results imported	Display dated results and changes over time	Not continuously measurable; test availability, sensitivity, specificity, and turnaround vary.
Sleep and workload	Wearable/diary	Contextual trends that may coincide with symptom or lab changes	Correlations do not establish causation or prove immune suppression.
Exposure / infection-control events	Structured log	Record reported contacts or environmental events if mission protocol permits	Not a substitute for microbiological testing or infection-control procedures.

3.4 Alert design
•	Use a symptom-first workflow: ask what changed, when it began, severity, and whether any mission-defined emergency symptom is present.
•	Use rules configured by the medical team. A prototype can demonstrate threshold-based routing, but it must not hard-code universal medical cut-offs without context.
•	Show 'possible concern—medical review required' rather than 'infection detected' when the evidence is a symptom or a nonspecific vital sign.
•	Allow clinicians to annotate laboratory values with interpretation and follow-up plans. Distinguish research biomarkers from routine clinical tests.
•	Track completion and sample provenance: collection time, assay, result time, and whether the sample was stored or processed under the required protocol.

3.5 Key findings
Finding	Detail
Herpesvirus reactivation in flight	Reactivation shown in Shuttle missions (10 to 16 days) and ISS missions of 180 days or more; viruses are shed in saliva (EBV, VZV, and HSV-1 per the review) and urine (CMV)
Frequency and magnitude with mission length	In a cohort of 23 astronauts on missions up to 180 days, shedding of EBV, VZV and CMV was detected in 8; reactivation increased in frequency, duration and amplitude relative to short Shuttle missions [verify cohort details in the original paper]
