4. Space radiation exposure and health effects
4.1 Why it matters
Space radiation includes galactic cosmic rays (GCR), solar energetic particles/solar particle events (SPEs), and trapped particles in planetary magnetic fields. The radiation field varies with mission trajectory, shielding, solar activity, spacecraft geometry, and time. Ionizing particles deposit energy in tissue and can damage DNA and other cellular structures. Potential outcomes include increased lifetime cancer risk, cataracts, cardiovascular and central nervous system concerns, and acute effects in sufficiently intense solar particle events. Risk estimates are uncertain, particularly for long-duration missions beyond low Earth orbit.
A critical distinction: astronauts cannot reliably sense radiation by symptoms. Radiation exposure is measured using dedicated instruments and mission models. A health app can display dosimeter readings and cumulative exposure, but it cannot infer a reliable radiation dose from a smartwatch, phone, skin temperature, or symptom checklist.

4.2 How exposure is measured
•	Absorbed dose is energy deposited per unit mass and is measured in gray (Gy).
•	Equivalent dose or operational dose-equivalent quantities account, in different ways, for radiation type/biological effectiveness and are commonly reported in sievert (Sv) or submultiples. The exact quantity must be stated; Gy and Sv are not interchangeable.
•	Active dosimeters can provide time-resolved measurements and may support alarms, depending on detector design and mission integration.
•	Passive dosimeters integrate exposure over time and are useful for cumulative records and independent verification, but often require specialized readout after collection.
•	Area monitors, personal dosimeters, spacecraft shielding models, solar-event information, and mission trajectory data may be combined by radiation-protection specialists. The app should preserve the origin and type of each value.

4.3 What the app can realistically monitor
Indicator	Source	App function	Limitations
Time-stamped dose-rate / dose-equivalent rate	Compatible active dosimeter or approved telemetry feed	Plot rate over time, identify spikes, show device status and last update	Instrument-specific; the app must not assume every device reports the same quantity.
Cumulative personal dose	Personal dosimeter record	Accumulate only compatible, non-duplicated readings; show trend and protocol-defined margin	Must preserve units and dosimetry quantity; uncertainty and calibration are important.
Passive dosimeter result	Authorized post-processing/import	Add cumulative mission value and measurement period to timeline	May not be available in real time; may represent an integrated period.
Solar particle event status	Mission-approved space-weather feed	Display alert status and approved sheltering/protection instructions	Requires a validated feed and operational authority; not predicted by health sensors.
Location/trajectory and shielding context	Mission navigation and spacecraft model, if authorized	Contextualize dose estimates and shelter events	Modelled dose is not a direct personal measurement; geometry and secondary particles matter.
Lifetime / mission exposure ledger	Validated dosimetry database and medical/radiation officer input	Show cumulative record, data provenance, uncertainty, and comparison with mission-specific limits	Do not use generic thresholds or independently declare a crew member safe/unsafe.

4.4 Protective measures the app can support
•	Display mission-approved radiation alerts and sheltering instructions from an authorized operational source.
•	Show the location of designated storm shelters and checklist status, if the mission interface supplies that information.
•	Track personal-dosimeter wear status and flag missing, stale, implausible, or disconnected data.
•	Visualize cumulative exposure alongside mission-specific thresholds supplied by radiation-protection staff; show uncertainty and measurement quantity.
•	Maintain an auditable log of alerts, acknowledgement, shelter actions, and dosimeter records.

5. Integrated app architecture
A feasible student prototype can be implemented as a responsive web/mobile dashboard backed by a structured database. Sensor hardware is optional for a first demonstrator; simulated and clearly labelled sample data can be used to test visualization, while the final interface should support real data import.
5.1 Suggested modules
•	Data intake: manual entry, CSV import, wearable export, lab-result import, and approved dosimeter data import. Every record includes timestamp, unit, source, quality flag, and provenance.
•	Validation layer: check missing values, impossible values, unit mismatches, duplicated records, stale readings, and device status before calculations.
•	Longitudinal storage: maintain personal baseline, mission day, pre-/in-flight/post-flight phase, and history of reviewed alerts.
•	Trend engine: calculate transparent summaries (daily/weekly averages where appropriate, change from baseline, completion rates, and missing-data indicators). Avoid opaque risk scores for the initial prototype.
•	Rules/alerts: clinician-configurable thresholds, severity, rationale, and response pathway. Log acknowledgements and suppress duplicate alerts without hiding persistent concerns.
•	Dashboard: domain cards, detailed trends, event timeline, data freshness, and a concise 'why this alert?' explanation.
•	Access and safety: role-based permissions, audit logs, encryption in transit and at rest where supported, export controls, and clear non-diagnostic labeling.

5.2 Example dashboard layout
Panel	Visible content	Example interaction
Overview	Mission day; last sync; data completeness; unresolved alerts	Tap an alert to see the exact source records.
Bone & muscle	Exercise adherence, activity/strength trends, imported BMD and labs	Compare a standardized strength test with personal baseline.
Immune & infection	Symptom timeline, temperature, imported CBC/assay results	Review symptom duration and clinician-defined escalation rule.
Radiation	Dosimeter dose/rate, cumulative ledger, solar-event status	Open measurement details, units, calibration/status, and last update.
Timeline	Exercise sessions, symptoms, labs, dosimeter alerts, interventions	Overlay events without claiming causation.

6. Validation and evaluation plan
A convincing prototype should be evaluated for data correctness and usability, not by claiming to predict astronaut disease. Use synthetic or de-identified test records unless authorized human-subjects approval and a clinical data pathway are available.
•	Data integrity: verify units, timestamps, record deduplication, missing-data handling, and cumulative-dose arithmetic using known test cases.
•	Visualization correctness: compare chart values and summaries against hand-calculated examples.
•	Alert logic: create test cases around each configured rule, including boundary values, stale inputs, and missing required data.
•	Usability: ask representative users to locate the latest result, explain why an alert fired, and identify what the app cannot conclude.
•	Safety: test that the system never labels a symptom as a confirmed infection, an activity decline as measured bone loss, or an unvalidated estimate as measured radiation dose.
•	Documentation: record the intended use, excluded uses, data sources, rule version, limitations, and change history.
7. Overall limitations and safety statement
This application is a educational prototype for organizing health indicators and supporting review. It is not a medical device unless separately developed, validated, and authorized under the applicable regulatory pathway. It does not diagnose disease, replace a flight surgeon, independently prescribe countermeasures, or supersede spacecraft safety and radiation-protection systems. Data from consumer wearables can be noisy and may perform differently in microgravity. Clinical tests and dosimetry data require validated equipment, standardized collection, calibration, quality control, and expert interpretation.



